#import "../lib.typ": *

#section-head(
  eyebrow: [What you need to know],
  title: [The MAHA Tracker Primer],
)

#el("div", class: "primer-grid reveal")[
  #el("div", class: "primer-body")[
    Make America Healthy Again coalesced as a political brand alongside the Trump administration, hitching a populist wellness agenda to a populist presidency. The movement blends real diagnoses with unreliable prescriptions, and a serious response requires separating the two. Some of its animating concerns are legitimate and long-overdue for mainstream attention: the epidemic of chronic disease, the dominance of ultra-processed foods in the American diet, and the long-documented capture of regulatory bodies like the FDA and USDA by the very industries they are meant to oversee. Other strands of the movement drift from evidence into speculation, or fold questions into conspiratorial framings that obscure more than they reveal. This site takes the legitimate concerns seriously, engages the harder questions on their merits, holds the administration accountable to the standard its own rhetoric invites, and points toward an #link("#alternative")[alternative democratic health vision] that can actually deliver on them.

    MAHA has pulled some long-marginalized issues into mainstream political conversation, and the advocates and supporters who arrived here, many of them parents, patients, and independent researchers with no simple partisan allegiance, deserve to be engaged, not dismissed. But engaging them seriously means following the evidence. And the record documented on this site is unambiguous: the current administration is not delivering even on the goals worth pursuing. Pesticide approvals have expanded. New PFAS-based chemicals have been greenlit for use on food crops. The agencies needed to enforce food safety standards have been staffed with former industry lobbyists and stripped of thousands of experienced personnel. The concerns are real; the vehicle is not.

    Whether or not you share sympathy for MAHA's political packaging, some underlying problems are well-documented and predate any administration. Americans consume more ultra-processed food than almost any nation on earth. Food dyes banned in the EU for decades remain common in U.S. products. PFAS contamination affects over 200 million people's drinking water. Childhood obesity and diet-related illness are undeniable crises.

    This site tracks _those specific policy issues_ (enacted laws, signed regulations, and executive actions), not the broader MAGA political agenda. We follow what passes and who passes it, regardless of party affiliation. The #link("#index")[index] and #link("#scorecard")[scorecard] document that record. They also point toward a conclusion: taking these concerns seriously requires an #link("#alternative")[alternative democratic health vision] grounded in robust public infrastructure, sustained federal investment, scientific rigor, and protections that are universal, enforceable, and inclusive of all Americans, not contingent on who holds power or which industries have the ear of the administration.
  ]
  #el("div", class: "primer-cards")[
    #nav-card("#scorecard", [Scorecard], aria: "Go to Partisan Scorecard")[
      A partisan breakdown of who is actually advancing food and environmental health policy, and who is blocking it, measured across enacted federal and state laws and regulations.
    ]
    #nav-card("#index", [Movement Timeline], aria: "Go to the Movement Timeline")[
      What the movement did and what was written about it — rallies, appointments, primaries, investigations — in date order, searchable by theme, arena or record type.
    ]
    #nav-card("#alternative", [Alt. Path], aria: "Go to Alternative Path section")[
      MAHA's mandate has been captured by corporate interests, but a bipartisan, policy-centered movement grounded in four clear principles offers a real alternative.
    ]
  ]
]
